  "recovery_latency_s": 124.56315279006958,
  "client_view_text": " I don\u2019t require code.\nIf a database system crashes during a write operation, the data may be inconsistent, leading to a difficult recovery process. A write-ahead log (WAL) helps prevent such issues by keeping a record of all writes to the database before they are applied to the main data storage. When a write operation is initiated, the database first writes it to the WAL, and only then to the main storage. In case of a crash, the database can recover by replaying the WAL entries, effectively \u201credoing\u201d the write operations that were in progress when the crash occurred. This ensures that the database is restored to a consistent state, even if the write operation was not completed.\nThe WAL also helps to prevent data loss in the event of a crash by keeping track of all transactions, including writes and deletes. This allows the database to \u201croll back\u201d incomplete transactions, restoring the database to a previous consistent state. By replaying the WAL entries, the database can reapply the original data, ensuring that the recovered data is consistent and correct.\nThe use of a WAL also enables the database to perform point-in-time recovery (PITR). PITR allows the database to recover to a specific point in time, rather than always recovering to the most recent check"
}